this is michael fuller  in 2004 he became britain's first and  only black chief constable  but to get there face battles no other  chief constable had to fight  despite the warnings michael persevered  and in 1975  after being brought up in care he left  to join the metropolitan police as a  cadet  everybody said there's no way you do  that then my biological parents said you  don't do that  my friends and school friends said no  way do you do that even my teachers i  mean everybody i looked up to said don't  join the police that's just not the  thing for a black person to do  in 1981  racial tensions led to violence in many  inner cities  and michael would find himself on a bus  in the middle of a fight between black  and blue  there was this quite sinister atmosphere  outside where there were black hues who  came to the side of the bus and started  taunting the police officers on the bus  and the police officers on the bus were  all white  and generally a lot older than me  but very bitter and twisted i felt and  used racist exclusives  one one of the issues was about  belonging that i didn't belong to the  people  uh the the group and the tribe on the  bus and i didn't feel like  i belonged to the tribe outside the bus  i didn't want to make an issue of it  quite the opposite i was not going to  let my color actually hinder me both in  terms of being promoted  and in terms of really  ensuring that i had a fulfilling career  he was successful setting up the racial  and violence crime task force in  response to the death of stephen  lawrence and as a commander spearheading  operation trident massively reducing gun  violence and black and black crime  but the racism would still continue  even when i was very senior i didn't  mean i escaped the racism  from even junior staff  but now he would have the power and says  this is why representation is still key  in terms of the things i'd like to see  improvements on i'd like to see  increased representation at the senior  levels of the police there haven't been  any other black chief constables  appointed since i was appointed as a  chief constable of a provincial police  force we need increased representation  at junior levels as well  because there's um  fewer black  people actually joining the police  than there are from other ethnic  minority groups  michael's journey was a long and hard  one but after becoming one of britain's  highest ranking officers he has taken  his place in history  shiman freeman powell sky news
 

